      {
        title: 'Emerging Frontiers and Research Directions',
        body: 'The peptide research landscape for muscle growth continues to evolve, with several areas of active investigation that may reshape the ranking in coming years.\n\n**Follistatin and Myostatin Inhibition**\n\nMyostatin inhibition remains one of the most compelling theoretical approaches to muscle growth, given the dramatic phenotypes observed in myostatin-deficient animals and the rare human cases of myostatin gene mutations. Beyond Follistatin 344, researchers are exploring AAV-mediated gene transfer of follistatin, follistatin-Fc fusion proteins, and small-molecule myostatin inhibitors. The peptide approach using Follistatin 344 offers the advantage of being a discrete, administerable research compound, though its stability and bioavailability in vivo remain areas requiring further characterisation.\n\n**Mitochondrial Peptides**\n\nThe discovery of mitochondrial-derived peptides (MDPs) such as MOTS-c and humanin has opened an entirely new category of endocrine signalling. MOTS-c is particularly interesting for muscle research because it appears to act as an exercise mimetic, activating AMPK and improving metabolic flexibility in skeletal muscle. Whether MDPs can produce measurable hypertrophic effects in isolation, or whether their primary role is in supporting the metabolic environment for growth, is an active research question.\n\n**Combination Growth Factor Protocols**\n\nSome researchers are exploring protocols that combine multiple direct growth factors — for example, pairing IGF-1 LR3 with a myostatin inhibitor, or combining IGF-1 LR3 with a GH secretagogue stack. The theoretical rationale is to activate anabolic signalling through multiple convergent pathways: the GH→IGF-1 axis (amplified upstream), direct IGF-1R activation (downstream bypass), and removal of negative regulation (myostatin inhibition). Such multi-pronged protocols require particularly careful monitoring but represent the cutting edge of peptide research for muscle growth.\n\n**Long-Acting GHRH Analogues**\n\nThe development of next-generation GHRH analogues with improved stability, selectivity, and pharmacokinetic profiles continues. Researchers are investigating modified GHRH peptides with enhanced resistance to enzymatic degradation, alternative DAC modifications with different albumin-binding kinetics, and peptides that target specific GHRH receptor splice variants. These developments may eventually offer researchers even greater control over the GH pulse profile with fewer dosing requirements.',
        table: {
